---

<Layout title="Privacy" description="How the x402Interop marketplace handles personal data." active="">
  <section class="phero">
    <div class="phero__inner">
      <h1>Privacy.</h1>
      <p class="phero__lede">DRAFT for legal review.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="legal">
    <dl class="legal__facts">
      <div>
        <dt>Last updated</dt>
        <dd>10 November 2026</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Operator</dt>
        <dd>THE3RDWEBLABS</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Covers</dt>
        <dd>Information the x402Interop marketplace handles when you browse, quote, or list.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Questions and requests</dt>
        <dd>
          <a href="https://github.com/the3rdweblabs/x402Interop">GitHub</a>
          {' · '}
          <a href="https://t.me/the3rdweblabs">Telegram</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2>1. What this notice covers</h2>
    <p>
      This notice describes the marketplace operated by THE3RDWEBLABS. It covers the site you
      are reading, the catalogue, deposit quotes, and the wallet signature used to change a
      listing. It does not cover an endpoint a seller runs, a wallet extension, a chain, or
      NEAR Intents. Those are separate services. The <a href="/terms">terms of use</a> describe
      what the marketplace is for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The marketplace does not ask you to create an account. It does not ask for your name, your
      email address, or a password.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Information the marketplace handles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Wallet details on your device.</strong> When you connect a wallet, the page
        stores the public address, the wallet name, the family (EVM, Sui, or Solana), and the
        networks that wallet reported. This stays in session storage in your browser under the
        key <code>x402interop.wallets</code>. The private key and the seed phrase are never sent
        to the marketplace and are not written into that storage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Listing content.</strong> A listing can include a name, a description, a
        resource URL, a documentation URL, a price, an asset, one or more networks, a payout
        address, a category, and the public address of the wallet that owns it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Signatures.</strong> A change to a listing is signed in your wallet. The
        facilitator keeps a single-use challenge for that action. The challenge expires after
        five minutes and can be used once. The stored result is the signature and the address
        it recovered, not a private key.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quote requests.</strong> Asking for a deposit quote sends the seller network,
        seller asset, seller address, amount, your network, your asset, your refund address,
        and the delivery mode. The facilitator returns a deposit address, an amount, and an
        expiry.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ordinary request data.</strong> The host that serves the site and the
        facilitator may record the time, the path, the IP address, and the user agent of a
        request, as part of running and protecting the service. The approved version of this
        notice will state how long those records are kept.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. What the marketplace does not do</h2>
    <p>
      The site does not use advertising cookies, cross-site tracking, or a third-party analytics
      product. It does not sell personal information. Connecting a wallet does not subscribe you
      to a mailing list, because the marketplace never receives an email address.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Why it is used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>To show the catalogue and open a listing.</li>
      <li>To build a deposit quote and show its status.</li>
      <li>To check that a listing is created, edited, or removed by the wallet that owns it.</li>
      <li>To keep the service available and to investigate abuse.</li>
      <li>To act on a valid takedown or legal request.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The approved version of this notice will name the legal ground for each of these uses.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Where it sits</h2>
    <p>
      Session storage stays on your device. It lasts until you disconnect the wallet, clear the
      site's data, or close the browser session. It is not sent to the operator as a stored
      profile.
    </p>
    <p>
      Listing content and quote records are stored by the facilitator so the catalogue and the
      payment status can be shown again. A challenge is discarded once it is used or once five
      minutes have passed. A record written to a public blockchain is outside this storage. The
      operator cannot edit or delete it.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Who else can see it</h2>
    <p>
      A published listing is visible to anyone who can open the catalogue, including the payout
      address and the owner address. A quote is visible to the person who requested it and to
      the facilitator that produced it.
    </p>
    <p>Beyond that, information leaves the marketplace only in these cases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your wallet.</strong> The signature prompt is shown by the wallet extension or
        app you chose. That wallet provider has its own notice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Settlement.</strong> A live quote is requested from NEAR Intents through 1Click.
        The request includes the networks, assets, addresses, and amount needed to price the
        swap.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hosting.</strong> The site and the facilitator run on infrastructure the
        operator chooses. That host processes the traffic required to serve the pages and the API.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Law and safety.</strong> The operator may disclose information when the law
        requires it, or when it is needed to investigate abuse of the service.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Public chains</h2>
    <p>
      A wallet address, a transaction, and a payout on a public chain can be read by anyone and
      cannot be deleted by the operator. Removing a listing from the marketplace removes it from
      the directory. It does not remove the address from the chain, and it does not hide a
      payment that has already been sent.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Your requests</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to see the listing data held for your address, to correct a listing you own,
      or to remove a listing you own. You can also ask about quote records tied to an address
      you control. Send the request through the GitHub repository or the Telegram contact above,
      and include the public address the request is about so the record can be found.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the law gives you a right to access, correct, export, or delete personal information,
      or to object to a use of it, you can use the same contact. The operator will answer within
      the time that law requires. A request cannot extend to data that lives only on a public
      chain, or to an endpoint the seller operates.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Children</h2>
    <p>
      The marketplace is not directed at anyone under 18. It does not ask for a date of birth.
      A listing that targets or exploits a minor is removed under the terms of use.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Listing changes are checked against a signature and a single-use challenge. Private keys
      are not collected. No method of storage or transmission is perfect. You should protect the
      device and the wallet you use to sign.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Changes</h2>
    <p>
      Changes to this notice are posted on this page. The date at the top changes with them.
      A change that alters what is collected, or who it is shared with, will be described here
      before it applies.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
